---
title: Heartbeat and Main Job Completion Handling
description: How Nx Cloud tracks main job completion and manages agent shutdown through the heartbeat process
filter: 'type:Guides'
topics:
  - 'Continuous integration'
---

### What is the heartbeat process?

A big challenge in building a task distribution engine is tracking when the main orchestrator job has finished (either successfully or due to an error).
For example, your agents might be busy running your Nx tasks, but GitHub Actions suddenly decides to kill your main job because it is consuming too many resources.
In that case, regardless of how you configured your pipeline or how many shutdown hooks we add to the code, we simply do not have enough time to tell Nx Cloud it can stop running tasks on agents.

To fix this, the first task-executing `nx` command on your main job (`nx run`, `nx run-many`, `nx affected`) starts a small background process that pings Nx Cloud every few seconds. If Nx Cloud does not receive a ping for 30 seconds, it assumes the main job has died, marks the run group complete, and shuts down its agents. Metadata commands such as `nx graph`, `nx show projects`, and `nx-cloud start-ci-run` do not start heartbeats on their own. They rely on a later task-executing command to do so.

Heartbeats are scoped to a run group, not to a CIPE. The run group is derived from `NX_CI_EXECUTION_ID` and, when set, `NX_CI_EXECUTION_ENV` (see the [environment variables reference](/docs/reference/environment-variables#nx-cloud-environment-variables)). Every invocation that resolves to the same run group shares a single heartbeat lifecycle, so an agent's heartbeat keeps the run group alive for the main job, and a parallel job on the same run group can also keep it alive or evict it, depending on when its own heartbeat stops. This scoping is what makes it possible to isolate one job's heartbeat from another job's without breaking distributed execution. See [isolate a job into its own run group](#isolate-a-job-into-its-own-run-group) below.

This is useful not just for worst-case scenarios, but it also keeps your CI config simple:

```yaml
# .nx/ci-config.yaml
dte:
  distribute-on: 5 linux-medium-js
```

```yaml
- run: npx nx start-nx-agents # provisions the agents that the affected tasks below are distributed to
- run: npm ci
- run: npx nx affected -t build,lint,test
# That's it - we don't need an extra step to tell NxCloud that we're done running Nx commands.
```

👆In the above case, once all the `affected` commands are executed and the main job shuts down, the heartbeat process will stop the pings. So, we'll assume the main job finished, and we can turn off the agents.

In summary, for 99% of cases, you will never have to think about the heartbeat or care that it exists. Your CI will just work.

### Isolate a job into its own run group

By default, every `nx` invocation in a single CI workflow run resolves to the same run group. That is what lets Nx Agents attach to their main coordinator without any per-job configuration. It also means that if a parallel job in the same workflow runs its own `nx` command, that job's heartbeat starts pinging the shared run group. When that job finishes, its heartbeat process goes with it. If no other heartbeat replaces it within 30 seconds, the run group is evicted and the main coordinator fails with `The Nx Cloud heartbeat process failed to report its status in time`, even though the coordinator itself is still running.

Set `NX_CI_EXECUTION_ENV` on the parallel job to move its `nx` invocations into a separate run group. The CIPE and the remote cache are still shared (cache entries are keyed by task hash, not by run group), but the heartbeat lifecycles are now independent, and that job can start and stop at its own pace without affecting the main coordinator.

```yaml
# .github/workflows/ci.yaml
jobs:
  main:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - run: npx nx-cloud start-ci-run --distribute-on="8 linux-medium-js" --stop-agents-after=e2e
      - run: npx nx affected -t lint,test,build,e2e

  static-checks:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    env:
      # Isolate this job's heartbeat from the main coordinator's run group.
      NX_CI_EXECUTION_ENV: ${{ github.job }}
    steps:
      - run: npx nx affected -t format:check,typecheck
```

Any distinct string works. `${{ github.job }}` is a convenient default because it is guaranteed unique across jobs in one workflow. The same env variable also pairs agents with the matching main job when you [have more than one main job](#multi-job-pipelines-with-different-stages), and selects the matching [`overrides`](/docs/reference/nx-cloud/ci-config#overrides) entry in `.nx/ci-config.yaml`, so pick a value that both is unique and reads well in configuration.

If you would rather have the parallel job opt out of Nx Cloud entirely (giving up cache reuse), pass `--no-cloud` on its `nx` commands instead. The isolation via `NX_CI_EXECUTION_ENV` is preferred when you still want the cache benefits.

### Caveats

In some specific cases, though, the heartbeat process will not work properly. In that case, turn off automatic completion and manage completion yourself. If you configure runs with `.nx/ci-config.yaml` and `nx-cloud start-nx-agents`, set [`lifecycle.heartbeat`](/docs/reference/nx-cloud/ci-config#lifecycleheartbeat) to `false`:

```yaml
# .nx/ci-config.yaml
dte:
  distribute-on: 5 linux-medium-js
lifecycle:
  heartbeat: false
```

If you configure runs with `nx-cloud start-ci-run` and CLI flags, pass [`--require-explicit-completion`](/docs/reference/nx-cloud-cli#nx-cloud-start-ci-run) instead. Both do the same thing, and both require you to add an `nx complete-ci-run` step that always runs:

```yaml
- run: npx nx-cloud start-ci-run --distribute-on="5 linux-medium-js" --require-explicit-completion
- run: npm ci
- run: npx nx affected -t build,lint,test
- run: npx nx complete-ci-run # this now tells NxCloud to turn off the agents
  if: always() # IMPORTANT: Always run, even in case of failures
```

The trade-off: if the `always()` step never runs (a catastrophic runner failure, a GitHub Actions outage that kills the whole job), the CIPE stays open until Nx Cloud's server-side reconciliation sweeps it, which can take hours. In practice `if: always()` runs after both success and failure, and even after workflow cancellation, so this is a good trade-off for most workflows.

When you might need to do this:

#### CI provider unexpectedly cleans up background processes

We've noticed that some CI providers tend to be more aggressive with background process management when moving between steps. Assume you have the following configuration:

```yaml
- run: npx nx start-nx-agents
- run: npm ci
- run: npx nx affected -t build,lint,test # This is the point where we turn on the heartbeat.
- run: ./deploy-my-projects.sh
- run: ./publish-test-results-to-sonarqube
- run: npx nx affected -t e2e
```

👆Notice how after running `npx nx affected -t build,lint,test`, we are doing some other work (deploying the projects, uploading test results, etc.). We've seen
some CI providers occasionally clean up background processes when moving between steps. So, if you see your main job failing when it gets to the `npx nx affected -t e2e` tests,
it might be because Nx Cloud thought the distribution had ended already, and it didn't accept any new Nx tasks.

The heartbeat process is especially vulnerable during these "transition phases" between steps.

To fix this, you can either manage completion explicitly (as mentioned above) or move all your Nx tasks into a single step or `nx` command. Both would fix the issue.

#### Multi-job pipelines with different stages

GitHub Actions supports defining dependencies between jobs. This allows you to create pipelines that spin up multiple machines at different stages, but that still run as part of the same overall "workflow".
Other providers allow you to do this too.

For example, you might want to:

1.  Spin up a job that runs some quick tasks such as formatting and linting.
2.  Once that's finished, create three machines for building and testing your app on Linux, macOS, and Windows.
3.  Finally, once those three machines finish, spin up a machine that deploys your app.

If Nx Cloud doesn't hear back from the heartbeat after a few seconds, it assumes something went wrong and fails the workflow.
When moving from one stage to the next, you need to turn off the first machine and wait for the next machines to boot up and start their heartbeats. This can cause you to go over the heartbeat threshold.

{% aside type="caution" title="Multi Machine/Job workflows" %}
Workflows involving multiple machines/jobs are the main source of heartbeat-related issues, simply because of how long it usually takes to restart the heartbeat after shutting it down.

{% /aside %}

The only fix in this scenario is to handle completion yourself and run `npx nx complete-ci-run` as the last command on your last machine in the pipeline.

### Heartbeat vs. `lifecycle.stop-after`

While both the heartbeat and `lifecycle.stop-after` tell Nx Cloud when it can shut down agents, they have different roles:

1.  `lifecycle.stop-after` is useful purely to avoid wasting unnecessary compute.
    - So, while you might still have agents actively running tasks, Nx Cloud can tell that you won't be sending it any more tasks in the future because of how you configured `lifecycle.stop-after`.
    - So, it can turn off any agents that are no longer running tasks.
    - [Read more about configuring `lifecycle.stop-after`](/docs/reference/nx-cloud/ci-config#lifecyclestop-after).
2.  The heartbeat, on the other hand, marks the completion of the main job.
    - It makes sure Nx Cloud instantly knows when the main job exited so it can update the status of its CI run.
    - In case of errors, it makes sure that it can instantly abandon any in-progress tasks.
